.\" Render with: groff -man -Tutf8 smithproxy-policy.7
.TH SMITHPROXY-POLICY 7 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
smithproxy-policy \- Smithproxy traffic matching and policy processing
.SH DESCRIPTION
Smithproxy policy is an ordered list of traffic rules. Each connection is
classified by named protocol, address and port objects. The first matching rule
selects an action and the profiles used for inspection, capture, DNS handling
and routing.
.PP
The short model is:
.nf
    connection metadata
            |
            v
    ordered policy match ---> action
            |
            +-- TLS / SSH / detection / content / DNS profiles
            `-- NAT and routing profile
.fi
.SH MATCH OBJECTS
Rules refer to named objects rather than embedding most values directly.
.TP
.B proto_objects
Map names to IP protocol numbers. Typical objects are TCP (6) and UDP (17).
.TP
.B address_objects
Contain IPv4/IPv6 CIDR ranges or FQDN values. FQDN matching depends on DNS data
known to Smithproxy and should not be treated as cryptographic identity.
.TP
.B port_objects
Contain inclusive port ranges from 0 through 65535.
.PP
An array in a rule matches any referenced object in that field. IPv4 and IPv6
catch-all objects are normally listed together when a rule should cover both
families.
.SH ORDER AND DEFAULTS
Rules are evaluated in configuration order. Put specific exceptions before
broad catch-all rules. Reordering rules can change both access decisions and
inspection behavior without changing any object or profile.
.PP
Every deployed policy should end in an intentional catch-all decision. Do not
rely on an undocumented implicit result when no rule matches. Review the active
policy in the CLI after configuration loading or schema upgrades.
.SH RULE FIELDS
.TP
.B proto
Name from
.BR proto_objects .
.TP
.B src, dst
Arrays of source and destination address-object names.
.TP
.B sport, dport
Arrays of source and destination port-object names.
.TP
.B action
.B accept
continues proxy processing;
.B deny
blocks the connection.
.B reject
is retained for legacy configuration compatibility.
.TP
.B nat
.B auto
uses translated upstream addressing.
.B none
requests transparent source handling where supported by the ingress mode,
kernel and routing configuration.
.TP
.B features
Optional feature tags recognized by the running build.
.TP
.B routing
Name of a routing entry, or
.BR none .
.SH PROFILE ASSIGNMENT
An accepted rule can attach named profiles. Empty profile names disable that
layer for the rule.
.TP
.B tls_profile
Controls TLS interception, certificate selection and validation. A bypass TLS
profile can explicitly pass encrypted TLS without terminating it.
.TP
.B ssh_profile
Enables SSHv2 MITM behavior on builds with libssh support.
.TP
.B detection_profile
Controls application engines and knowledge-base metadata collection.
.TP
.B content_profile
Controls payload capture, byte limits and content processing.
.TP
.B alg_dns_profile
Controls DNS request/response association, ID handling and caching.
.PP
Profiles are independent building blocks. For example, an accepted connection
can use TLS inspection without payload capture, or capture opaque TCP without a
protocol detection engine.
.SH ROUTING
Routing profiles affect the upstream leg after policy selection. They can:
.IP \(bu 2
rewrite the destination address and port;
.IP \(bu 2
select among multiple targets using round-robin, sticky-L3 or sticky-L4;
.IP \(bu 2
rewrite an exact outbound TLS SNI value.
.PP
Routing does not rewrite the metadata originally observed on the client-facing
leg. Diagnostics should therefore distinguish requested and effective upstream
destinations.
.SH TRANSPARENCY AND NAT
With
.B nat = auto
the upstream socket normally uses a Smithproxy host address. With transparent
source handling, the upstream leg can use the original client address. This
requires Linux transparent-socket support and policy routing which returns
server traffic to Smithproxy.
.PP
Incorrect transparent routing commonly produces a successful client-side
accept followed by an upstream timeout or asymmetric return path. Test routing
independently from TLS or content inspection.
.SH DNS AND FQDN MATCHING
FQDN policy objects use names learned through DNS inspection and caches. A
single address can serve multiple names, and cached data can become stale.
TLS SNI or HTTP host metadata can provide additional context but does not turn
an address rule into proof of application identity.
.PP
Place security-critical name rules behind appropriate certificate validation
and define an explicit fallback for connections whose name is unknown.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Specific accept followed by default deny
.nf
    address_objects = {
        clients = { type = "cidr"; value = "192.0.2.0/24"; };
        any4 = { type = "cidr"; value = "0.0.0.0/0"; };
    };
    port_objects = {
        https = { start = 443; end = 443; };
        all = { start = 0; end = 65535; };
    };

    policy = (
        {
            proto = "tcp";
            src = [ "clients" ]; dst = [ "any4" ];
            sport = [ "all" ]; dport = [ "https" ];
            action = "accept"; nat = "auto";
            tls_profile = "default";
            detection_profile = "detect";
            content_profile = "metadata_only";
            routing = "none";
        },
        {
            proto = "tcp";
            src = [ "any4" ]; dst = [ "any4" ];
            sport = [ "all" ]; dport = [ "all" ];
            action = "deny"; nat = "auto"; routing = "none";
        }
    );
.fi
.PP
The HTTPS rule matches first. Other TCP traffic reaches the final deny rule.
Equivalent IPv6 objects must be included when IPv6 is allowed.
.SS TLS bypass before general inspection
.nf
    policy = (
        { proto = "tcp"; dst = [ "software_updates" ];
          dport = [ "https" ]; action = "accept";
          tls_profile = "bypass"; nat = "auto"; routing = "none"; ... },
        { proto = "tcp"; dst = [ "any", "any6" ];
          dport = [ "https" ]; action = "accept";
          tls_profile = "default"; nat = "auto"; routing = "none"; ... }
    );
.fi
.PP
If these rules are reversed, the broad rule wins and the intended bypass is
never used. Ellipses represent required match fields omitted for readability.
.SS Route one service to an alternate upstream
.nf
    routing = {
        maintenance = {
            dnat_address = [ "maintenance_hosts" ];
            dnat_port = [ "https" ];
            dnat_lb_method = "sticky-l3";
        };
    };

    policy = (
        { proto = "tcp"; dst = [ "public_service" ];
          dport = [ "https" ]; action = "accept";
          nat = "auto"; routing = "maintenance"; ... }
    );
.fi
.PP
Client-visible destination metadata remains the original service; the routing
profile selects the effective destination of the upstream leg.
.SH CLI WORKFLOW
Useful commands include:
.nf
    show config policy
    show config policy <index>
    show config objects
    diag proxy policy list
    configure terminal
    edit policy
    edit [0]
    save config
    execute reload
.fi
.PP
Use contextual completion for object and profile names. After changes, verify
both the configured policy and a live session: a syntactically valid rule can
still match more or less traffic than intended.
.SH TESTING POLICY
Since October 2026, Smithproxy uses a strict documented validation policy with
commit-specific reports under
.IR docs/test-reports/ .
Policy results should include positive matches, adjacent non-matches, ordering
conflicts and reload behavior rather than only the intended happy path.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Policy order is security-sensitive. A broad accept rule above a deny or bypass
exception makes the lower rule unreachable. Conversely, a broad MITM rule can
decrypt traffic that should remain private or break pinned applications.
.PP
Keep policy changes reviewable: use descriptive object/profile names, avoid
overlapping ranges where possible, document intentional bypasses and validate
representative IPv4, IPv6, explicit-proxy and transparent flows.
